WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:13.030
<v Speaker 1>Welcome, and as you join us today to explore Reza Shakazemi's spiritual ethos, we arrive at the letter's close, where the whole edifice of just rule turns out to rest on something done alone in the dead of night.

2
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.430
<v Speaker 2>It is such a stark image to begin with, really.

3
00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.310
<v Speaker 1>Know that your every act is dependent upon your prayer.

4
00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:20.910
<v Speaker 2>That single line.

5
00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:25.790
<v Speaker 2>I mean, it completely reframes everything we have discussed in this deep dive so far.

6
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:26.750
<v Speaker 1>It really does.

7
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:36.270
<v Speaker 2>Because if you manage a team or run a business or even just try to keep a household from falling apart, we usually think about governance purely in terms of visible systems.

8
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:42.750
<v Speaker 2>And up until now, the Imam, peace be upon him, has spent this entire letter counseling the governor

9
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.230
<v Speaker 2>He counsels on those very intricate, highly visible mechanics of rule.

10
00:00:47.470 --> 00:01:01.550
<v Speaker 1>He has laid out these incredibly rigorous methods, methods for weighing the different classes of society against one another, detailed instructions for appointing judges, managing the affairs of merchants, directing the conduct of soldiers.

11
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.910
<v Speaker 2>Those profound warnings about restraining the ruler's own appetites.

12
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.790
<v Speaker 1>All of that forms the outward face of justice.

13
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.950
<v Speaker 1>It's the visible architecture of placing everything in its objective order in the physical world.

14
00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:19.150
<v Speaker 2>But most manuals of statecraft, they stop entirely at that visible architecture.

15
00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.870
<v Speaker 1>They do because the political mind desperately wants metrics it can track.

16
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:24.350
<v Speaker 2>For sure.

17
00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:31.390
<v Speaker 2>It wants ledgers and council chambers and observable policies, systems that can be audited by committees.

18
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:39.950
<v Speaker 1>We tend to build societies on this assumption that if the external scaffolding is strong enough, the building will just, it will stand regardless of who occupies it.

19
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.710
<v Speaker 2>But the central crisis presented here shatters that assumption completely.

20
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:44.510
<v Speaker 1>It does.

21
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:51.150
<v Speaker 1>The argument is that this entire visible architecture is profoundly, terrifyingly vulnerable.

22
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:54.190
<v Speaker 2>Because every measured judgment, every honest appointment.

23
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:56.030
<v Speaker 1>Restraint of executive power.

24
00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.190
<v Speaker 2>It all relies on an invisible foundation.

25
00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.510
<v Speaker 2>I mean, good intentions are completely insufficient here.

26
00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:01.670
<v Speaker 1>Clever policies are.

27
00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:02.430
<v Speaker 1>insufficient.

28
00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:07.550
<v Speaker 1>The capacity to place things rightly in the physical world must be drawn down from a higher source.

29
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.350
<v Speaker 2>Which brings us to the ultimate stakes of this text.

30
00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.990
<v Speaker 2>What happens when the ruler is unwatched?

31
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.350
<v Speaker 1>That is the crisis.

32
00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:22.430
<v Speaker 1>What sustains justice when there is no ledger recording the action and no public observing the decision?

33
00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:25.150
<v Speaker 2>When there is no immediate consequence for selfishness.

34
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:31.350
<v Speaker 1>If justice is merely a performance for the public, it collapses the very moment the public is removed.

35
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.830
<v Speaker 2>We see that all the time, don't we?

36
00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.550
<v Speaker 2>Leaders who are brilliant in public but become entirely different people behind closed doors.

37
00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.150
<v Speaker 1>Which introduces the first governing analogy we really need to sit with.

38
00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.310
<v Speaker 1>The text presents prayer as a fountain overflowing into every action.

39
00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:45.870
<v Speaker 2>A fountain.

40
00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:46.630
<v Speaker 2>I like that.

41
00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.070
<v Speaker 1>The inward practice is the hidden spring from which all public good flows.

42
00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:55.190
<v Speaker 2>So governance drawn from this spring is qualitatively different from

43
00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:58.510
<v Speaker 2>something just manufactured by political calculation.

44
00:02:58.670 --> 00:02:59.870
<v Speaker 1>Inherently different.

45
00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:05.470
<v Speaker 1>And to understand how this overflow functions, we have to look closely at how the ruler connects to this source.

46
00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.350
<v Speaker 1>The phrase used is purifying one's religion.

47
00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:16.510
<v Speaker 2>I feel like on the surface, purifying one's religion might sound to a modern ear like, well, just a simple instruction to avoid hypocrisy.

48
00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:21.630
<v Speaker 1>Like a basic directive to ensure public rituals aren't performed just for applause.

49
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:24.270
<v Speaker 2>Just a warning against showing off.

50
00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.390
<v Speaker 1>But it reaches far deeper into the human interior than that.

51
00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:33.390
<v Speaker 1>To purify one's religion is to purify the very substance of the soul's relationship to God.

52
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:36.710
<v Speaker 2>The text insists on this really radical premise, doesn't it?

53
00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:45.070
<v Speaker 1>Insists that no virtue, whether it is justice, patience, or mercy, can rise higher than the soul's nearness to the source of that virtue.

54
00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:49.150
<v Speaker 2>A virtue is only as real and only as durable as the relationship that feeds it.

55
00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.310
<v Speaker 1>That shifts the entire ethical framework from a horizontal

56
00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:53.950
<v Speaker 1>to a vertical one.

57
00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.830
<v Speaker 1>Ethics is no longer just a list of rules to memorize.

58
00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.750
<v Speaker 2>Or a social contract you can negotiate.

59
00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.990
<v Speaker 2>It becomes a measure of proximity to the divine.

60
00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:05.550
<v Speaker 1>Let's define this proximity because the tradition provides a highly specific concept here.

61
00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.070
<v Speaker 1>The term is takorob.

62
00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.190
<v Speaker 1>It represents cultivating a right vision in prayer.

63
00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:14.830
<v Speaker 2>And this cultivation is the only way right action is generated in the physical world.

64
00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:21.230
<v Speaker 1>Because right action does not just conjure itself out of a vacuum, nor is it sustained by willpower alone.

65
00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:22.310
<v Speaker 2>It flows from a right vision.

66
00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:25.310
<v Speaker 1>This vision is cultivated in prayer or nowhere at all.

67
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:31.630
<v Speaker 1>The Quran itself states that reciting the book and establishing the prayer restrains from indecency and wrongdoing.

68
00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.670
<v Speaker 2>It places drawing near as the absolute root of all ethical restraint.

69
00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:43.870
<v Speaker 1>Which reveals why the obligatory prayers are so heavily emphasized in the life of a leader, they act as appointed interruptions.

70
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:52.830
<v Speaker 2>Because A ruler's life, by definition, is completely consumed by the ceaseless, crushing demands of administration.

71
00:04:52.990 --> 00:05:03.070
<v Speaker 1>The fixed, recurring prayers force the ruler to physically leave the business of statecraft, to step away from the council chamber and stand defenseless before the one to whom they must ultimately answer.

72
00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.070
<v Speaker 2>Defenseless is the operative word there.

73
00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.230
<v Speaker 2>The posture of power is dismantled.

74
00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.430
<v Speaker 1>But, and this is vital, it is not a passive defenselessness.

75
00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:14.350
<v Speaker 1>The text places striking, almost alarming physical demands on this.

76
00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:15.550
<v Speaker 2>It really does.

77
00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:29.470
<v Speaker 1>The letter says, give unto God of your vital energy in your nights and your days, and perform fully that by which you draw near to God, doing so perfectly, without becoming dull or deficient, taking your body to its limits.

78
00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.110
<v Speaker 2>The physical intensity described there is severe.

79
00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:38.350
<v Speaker 2>I mean, this is not a request for a few minutes of quiet contemplation to lower your blood pressure before a stressful meeting.

80
00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:38.790
<v Speaker 1>Not at all.

81
00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.150
<v Speaker 2>It demands one's vital energy.

82
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.710
<v Speaker 2>It speaks of taking the body to its limits.

83
00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:51.270
<v Speaker 1>An immense expenditure of effort across the days and the nights, even when the demands of the state are already exhausting the individual.

84
00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.950
<v Speaker 2>Which brings in the third governing analogy, driving home this theme of relentless effort.

85
00:05:56.190 --> 00:06:02.390
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the imam states that prayer without strenuous exertion is like arrows loosed without a bow.

86
00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:04.110
<v Speaker 2>That is an unforgiving image.

87
00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.630
<v Speaker 1>It forces us to confront the physics of spiritual intention.

88
00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.590
<v Speaker 1>Gesture without tension cannot hit the mark.

89
00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:14.430
<v Speaker 2>Because motion without the instrument that gives it force achieves absolutely nothing.

90
00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.310
<v Speaker 1>An arrow thrown by hand simply falls to the ground.

91
00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:20.670
<v Speaker 1>Sincerity demands the undivided integration of the soul.

92
00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:31.150
<v Speaker 2>Gathering all those scattered energies, all the anxieties and divided attentions of a ruling mind into a single piercing orientation that takes immense labor.

93
00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:38.150
<v Speaker 1>A fragmented soul cannot offer a sincere prayer because sincerity is defined here as the undividedness of the one who prays.

94
00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.270
<v Speaker 2>I want to build on that physics analogy for a second.

95
00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:51.550
<v Speaker 2>We are essentially talking about the difference between moving heavy weights in a gym and just pantomiming the motion in the air.

96
00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.830
<v Speaker 2>If you just go through the motions of a bicep curl,

97
00:06:55.430 --> 00:07:00.590
<v Speaker 2>Without holding any weight, your muscle never tears, it never rebuilds, it never actually strengthens.

98
00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.470
<v Speaker 1>The resistance is what creates the transformation.

99
00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:06.590
<v Speaker 2>But this raises a critical question about the outcome of all this labor.

100
00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:13.790
<v Speaker 2>If the ruler takes their body to its limits, drawing the bow with everything they have, sweating through this internal resistance,

101
00:07:14.230 --> 00:07:16.110
<v Speaker 2>What is the resulting transformation?

102
00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.870
<v Speaker 1>What actually happens to the practitioner?

103
00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:18.270
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

104
00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:24.030
<v Speaker 1>To answer that, the text introduces the teaching of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

105
00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:30.350
<v Speaker 1>The text points to a luminous, highly celebrated saying often referred to as the Hadith of drawing near.

106
00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:33.110
<v Speaker 1>In this saying, God speaks in the first person.

107
00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:35.470
<v Speaker 2>The structure of that saying is mesmerizing.

108
00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.670
<v Speaker 1>It fundamentally distinguishes between two types of devotion.

109
00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.830
<v Speaker 1>First, God declares that the slave draws near through nothing.

110
00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.070
<v Speaker 1>He loves more than a

111
00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:45.310
<v Speaker 1>obligatory acts.

112
00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:51.390
<v Speaker 2>So the obligatory acts, the required foundational duties, they establish the baseline relationship.

113
00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:54.910
<v Speaker 1>But then the saying moves further into uncharted territory.

114
00:07:55.150 --> 00:08:00.350
<v Speaker 1>It states that the slave never ceases to draw near through supererogatory acts until God loves him.

115
00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:09.710
<v Speaker 2>For anyone listening who might be unfamiliar with the term, supererogatory acts are those freely offered devotions undertaken entirely beyond all basic requirements.

116
00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:10.270
<v Speaker 1>They are.

117
00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.070
<v Speaker 2>It is the spiritual equivalent of staying hours after your shift ends.

118
00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:19.230
<v Speaker 2>just to help a colleague simply out of love for the work rather than just showing up to fulfill a contract.

119
00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:23.710
<v Speaker 1>The freely offered devotion deepens the relationship into intense intimacy.

120
00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:35.950
<v Speaker 1>And then comes the astonishing claim at the heart of the saying, God says, and when I love him, I am his hearing by which he hears, his sight by which he sees, his hand by which he grasps, and his foot by which he walks.

121
00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:37.630
<v Speaker 2>I have to push back here.

122
00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:39.230
<v Speaker 2>I mean, that sounds like wordplay.

123
00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:42.910
<v Speaker 2>Isn't God becomes his hand just a fancy way of saying he acts well?

124
00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:44.830
<v Speaker 1>You think it's just a metaphor?

125
00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:52.150
<v Speaker 2>It feels like a battiful, metaphorical flourish to describe someone who has developed really solid moral habits.

126
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.190
<v Speaker 2>We use language like this all the time to describe someone who is simply very focused, you know?

127
00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.390
<v Speaker 1>I understand the instinct to reduce it to metaphor.

128
00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:05.990
<v Speaker 1>The modern mind wants to domesticate language like this to make it merely symbolic so it fits neatly into our material

129
00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:07.070
<v Speaker 1>realist worldview.

130
00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.430
<v Speaker 2>It is more comfortable that way.

131
00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.510
<v Speaker 1>But the text does not permit that reduction.

132
00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:14.590
<v Speaker 1>It demands we read this as a profound ontological reality.

133
00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.270
<v Speaker 2>An actual shift in being.

134
00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:21.150
<v Speaker 1>The ego is literally a face through the physical and spiritual act of prostration.

135
00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:27.550
<v Speaker 1>When the text commands the self to bow down, it is describing a self-effacement that creates a vacuum.

136
00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:31.390
<v Speaker 2>A vacuum that allows the divine presence to become the very faculty of perception.

137
00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:33.390
<v Speaker 1>The text means exactly what it says.

138
00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:35.230
<v Speaker 1>His hearing by which he hears.

139
00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:35.990
<v Speaker 1>His sight.

140
00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:43.470
<v Speaker 2>We are talking about a fundamental alteration in how a human being perceives reality then.

141
00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:46.830
<v Speaker 2>It's not just about choosing to do good things.

142
00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:51.310
<v Speaker 1>It is about the optical lens through which the world is viewed being completely swapped out.

143
00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:51.870
<v Speaker 1>I see.

144
00:09:52.190 --> 00:10:01.670
<v Speaker 1>To see things as they truly are rather than through the distorted funhouse mirror of one's own ego, insecurities and appetites is the absolute prerequisite for justice.

145
00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:04.510
<v Speaker 2>Think about how a selfish leader views a forest.

146
00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:07.070
<v Speaker 2>They just see lumber and profit.

147
00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:09.950
<v Speaker 1>And A terrified leader sees a place for enemies to hide.

148
00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:16.510
<v Speaker 2>Only a leader whose perception has been clarified can see the forest for what it actually is in the grand order of creation.

149
00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:21.390
<v Speaker 1>If you cannot see a thing as it truly is, you cannot possibly place it where it truly belongs.

150
00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:25.630
<v Speaker 1>And placing each thing according to what it truly is defines perfect justice.

151
00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:28.750
<v Speaker 2>That completely changes the center of gravity of a whole life.

152
00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:33.470
<v Speaker 2>The rigid boundaries we usually draw between private devotion and public work just dissolve here.

153
00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.910
<v Speaker 2>We usually think of a leader's schedule as a zero-sum game.

154
00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:43.710
<v Speaker 2>An hour spent in prayer is an hour stolen from managing the economy or listening to the grievances of the citizens.

155
00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:47.950
<v Speaker 1>The logic of the overflow makes that zero-sum thinking obsolete.

156
00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:48.830
<v Speaker 2>How so?

157
00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:55.150
<v Speaker 1>If a ruler gives God the most excellent, undivided portion of their time, they do not have less capacity for the people.

158
00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:57.150
<v Speaker 1>They actually have infinitely more.

159
00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:03.630
<v Speaker 1>They bring the gathered quality of nearness, that clarified perception, directly to their subjects.

160
00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.710
<v Speaker 2>So the private practice and the public good

161
00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:08.830
<v Speaker 2>are not competing for a fixed number of hours on a schedule.

162
00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.630
<v Speaker 1>The hidden spring feeds the public action.

163
00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:17.790
<v Speaker 1>A ruler who spends the night gathering their soul into unity will not spend the day tearing the state into factions.

164
00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:25.790
<v Speaker 2>Having established that the overflow of nearness transforms perception itself, we really need to examine the mechanics of this transformation.

165
00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:26.270
<v Speaker 1>We do.

166
00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.850
<v Speaker 2>Nearness sustained amidst the chaos of governing?

167
00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:35.970
<v Speaker 2>What keeps the perception clear when 1,000 different crisis points are screaming for the ruler's attention?

168
00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.410
<v Speaker 1>Know that your every act is dependent upon your prayer.

169
00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:42.890
<v Speaker 2>We are shifting to the specific method of maintaining that clarity.

170
00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:48.570
<v Speaker 1>The text introduces the specific supererogatory devotion at the heart of this nearness.

171
00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:51.050
<v Speaker 1>The text uses the term Dipkar.

172
00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.170
<v Speaker 1>It functions as both a methodic practice

173
00:11:54.570 --> 00:12:00.810
<v Speaker 1>the deliberate, rhythmic, repeated invocation of divine names, and a permanent condition of awareness.

174
00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:07.290
<v Speaker 2>It is the concentrated act performed in isolation and the abiding condition carried into the crowded marketplace simultaneously.

175
00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:13.730
<v Speaker 1>It is the striking of the match in the dark room and the residual warmth that fills the room long after the initial friction.

176
00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.610
<v Speaker 2>Which leads us to the second governing analogy of the text.

177
00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.850
<v Speaker 1>The text compares the human heart to a mirror or a blade.

178
00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:21.610
<v Speaker 2>Through the friction of daily life,

179
00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:28.650
<v Speaker 2>Through the accumulation of minor compromises, distractions, and neglect, this mirror becomes dulled and clouded with oxidation.

180
00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:30.250
<v Speaker 1>Remembrance is the polish.

181
00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.650
<v Speaker 1>It is the friction applied to burnish the heart until it recovers senses it had lost.

182
00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:39.370
<v Speaker 2>Through this polishing, the heart achieves hearing after deafness and seeing after blindness.

183
00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:44.170
<v Speaker 1>There is a crucial distinction to draw out regarding how this polish works.

184
00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:48.010
<v Speaker 1>Remembrance is not the acquisition of new information.

185
00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.530
<v Speaker 2>That is such a vital point.

186
00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:55.050
<v Speaker 2>The entire modern educational paradigm is based on forward accumulation.

187
00:12:55.290 --> 00:13:00.570
<v Speaker 1>We assume learning means moving forward, downloading facts we did not possess yesterday.

188
00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:02.690
<v Speaker 2>Adding new tools to our mental toolbox.

189
00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:07.370
<v Speaker 2>The text describes remembrance operating uniquely with time.

190
00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:09.210
<v Speaker 2>It moves downward and backward.

191
00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:11.530
<v Speaker 1>It is fundamentally an act of recovery.

192
00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:16.410
<v Speaker 2>Think of it like running a defragmentation or a factory reset on a heavily corrupted computer.

193
00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:19.930
<v Speaker 2>You are not installing new software to fix the glitches.

194
00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:27.290
<v Speaker 2>You are stripping away all the accumulated malware, all the junk files, all the fragmented data that have bogged down the system over the years.

195
00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:31.690
<v Speaker 1>In order to uncover the pristine operating system that was there from the moment the machine was built.

196
00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:32.970
<v Speaker 2>It uncovers what was forgotten.

197
00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.930
<v Speaker 2>a new reflective surface onto the metal blade.

198
00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:41.290
